## Prompt 4 — Fix tune collection data loading and build compact shared tune primitives

```text
Work in the current Tunes App repository after Prompts 1–3. Read AGENTS.md and the audit, preserve existing changes and treat current code/schema as authoritative. Do not edit sources/. Implement, migrate and verify this workstream. Do not redesign all consuming screens yet.

Goal: eliminate unbounded phone datasets and create the shared tune-list architecture needed by Catalogue, Known, Practice, Lists, Profiles and Compare.

Implement the following:

1. Audit loadLibraryData and every query that currently fetches separate desktop/mobile tune collections. Remove the pattern where phone receives every matching tune while desktop receives a bounded page.
2. Create a server-side cursor or otherwise stable pagination contract for tune collections. It must support search, filters, sort/group selection, result count where affordable, and a deterministic tie-breaker. Avoid offset pagination if it will become unstable at this data size.
3. Keep filter/search/group state in validated URL parameters so results are shareable and browser back/forward works. Do not expose private identifiers unnecessarily.
4. Build shared TuneIdentity and TuneRow primitives. A compact row should support title, useful alias, tune type/style, key, source/provenance summary, personal state and a small number of contextual actions without nested interactive semantics.
5. Build a shared paginated collection controller for loading, empty, error, retry, next/previous or load-more behaviour. Avoid giant client-side arrays and huge accessibility trees.
6. Provide adapters/query helpers for Catalogue, Known, Practice, Learning Queue, list membership, Profile repertoire and Compare. Each consumer may have different permissions and fields; do not force private data through a public shape.
7. Retain CardPager only for genuinely small finite sets such as a short active practice queue or badge carousel. Add a clear size guard or documentation so it is not reused for a catalogue.
8. Remove per-row forms where a single event/action handler can safely serve the collection. Preserve server-side permission checks.
9. Add query/index review and tests. If a database index or migration is truly necessary, make the smallest reversible migration, document why, and retain existing data/constraints. Do not weaken row-level security.
10. Measure representative result size, query count and rendered item count before and after. Include these numbers in the completion report.

Acceptance criteria:

- No phone route fetches every matching tune merely to render a list/pager.
- Shared pagination is deterministic and covered by tests, including duplicate sort values and empty/final pages.
- TuneRow has clean link/button semantics and works with keyboard and screen readers.
- Search/filter URL state survives reload and back/forward.
- Existing tune permissions and actions remain correct.
- Build and data-layer tests pass, with no accidental N+1 media/list/status queries.
```

